  "id": "Sutton-DM2026_00199",
  "appeal_status": null,
  "appeal_start_date": null,
  "description": "Demolition of the existing building and erection of a two storey building with accommodation in the roof to provide 6 self contained dwellings. Provision of an air source heat pump, cycle and refuse storage and asssociated landscaping.",
  "development_type": "Minor dwellings",
  "decision_conditions": [
    "The proposed development, by virtue of its excessive density (6 units), scale, and massing, represents a significant overdevelopment of a constrained corner plot. The building is disproportionately large for the site, featuring an over-proliferation of dormers that create a top-heavy, three storey appearance wholly discordant with the established two storey urban grain and modest Victorian character of the St John's Road Area of Special Local Character (ASLC). The proposal projects significantly beyond the established building line on St James Road, closing essential visual gaps and appearing visually intrusive. This intensified use fails to respect the local context and the proposal is therefore contrary to Policies 28 and 30 of the Sutton Local Plan (2018) and Policy D3 of the London Plan (2021).",
    "The proposal would result in detrimental impacts on the living conditions of neighbouring occupiers. The excessive height and width of the building in such close proximity to 59 St John's Road and 54 St James Road would result in an oppressive sense of enclosure and a significant loss of outlook. Furthermore, the intensive arrangement of unobscured first and second floor dormer windows would result in unacceptable direct and perceived overlooking toward 58 St James Road and the private amenity spaces of adjacent properties. The proposal is therefore contrary to Policy 29 of the Sutton Local Plan (2018).",
    "The development proposes zero on-site car parking in an area where peak overnight parking stress already exceeds 85%, indicating a saturated parking environment. The intensification of the site to provide six dwellings would result in a high risk of overspill parking on the narrow residential highway. This additional demand would exacerbate existing parking pressures, leading to hazardous conditions for all road users and adversely affecting highway and pedestrian safety, particularly near a busy junction. The proposal is therefore contrary to Policies 36 and 37 of the Sutton Local Plan (2018) and Policies T6 and T6.1 of the London Plan (2021).",
    "The proposal fails to adequately address critical environmental risks associated with the development. Due to the sites historical use as an industrial machine shop and engineering workshop, there is a high potential for existing contamination. In the absence of a Phase 1 Contaminated Land Desk Study and preliminary risk assessment at the application stage, the LPA cannot be satisfied that the site is suitable for a sensitive residential use. In addition, the application proposes multiple air source heat pumps in close proximity to residential boundaries. Without a supporting BS4142 noise assessment, the applicant has failed to demonstrate that the mechanical plant would not result in an unacceptable acoustic impact and disturbance to neighbouring residents. The proposal is therefore contrary to Policies 29 and 34 of the Sutton Local Plan (2018)."
  ],
